  <h2 id="what">What it is</h2>
  <p>A journal is one ordered log. A writer appends batches of opaque records, and readers read
  them in order. Paxos orders the data itself, in every slot, not only the metadata. The journal
  uses the roles of <a href={url("paxos/compartmentalized/")}>Compartmentalized Paxos</a>, and
  changes its acceptors with <a href={url("paxos/matchmaker/")}>Matchmaker Paxos</a>. One
  consensus leader orders the whole journal, so the throughput of that leader is the limit of v1
  (<a href={`${J}/01-goal.md`}>§J1</a>).</p>
  <p>The journal knows ids only. Each journal has its own matchmaker set, its own controller and
  its own table of segments. Names, tenants and cells belong to the service above it, <a href={url("parosd/")}>parosd</a>. The journal is a library first: one process can host many
  journals.</p>

  <div class="arch" role="img" aria-label="One journal. A client sends its calls to the leader. The leader gives each write a slot, and proxy leaders send Phase 2 to the acceptor grid of the segment in force. The chosen slots go to the replica set of that segment, which applies them and serves reads. Beside it, the matchmakers of the journal hold the configuration of each ballot and the register of segment records. The controller writes to the register only, with its fenced reshape call.">
    <div class="cellframe">
      <span class="cellname">one journal</span>
      <div class="box">client <small>Write · Read · Truncate · SetLeader</small></div>
      <div class="arrow">▼</div>
      <div class="row">
